13. Charge Architecture

13.1 Charger view

The charger must see:
	1.	Main pack negative
	2.	Main pack positive
	3.	All intermediate cell taps

That means the charger sees the installed battery as:

One 10S LiPo pack

not as two separate 5S packs.

⸻

13.2 Charging ports required

Port	Required	Function
Main charge leads	Yes	Carries bulk charge current
10S balance leads	Yes	Monitors and balances all cells


⸻

14. Why Main Leads Alone Are Not Enough

A charger connected only to pack negative and pack positive can see only total voltage.

That is not sufficient to ensure:
	•	no individual cell exceeds max voltage
	•	no drift between cell groups
	•	correct end-of-charge on every cell

14.1 Simple comparison table

Method	Sees total pack voltage	Sees each cell	Safe for routine LiPo balance charging
Main leads only	Yes	No	No
Main + balance leads	Yes	Yes	Yes


⸻

15. Build Procedure

Step 1 — Select packs

Choose two 5S packs that are as closely matched as possible.

Acceptance checks
	•	both physically undamaged
	•	resting voltages close
	•	no swelling
	•	no damaged balance leads
	•	same connector family preferred

⸻

Step 2 — Define external connectors

Choose:
	•	main connector type
	•	balance connector format
	•	service disconnect / arming connector if used

Recommendation
	•	XT90 for main if current is substantial
	•	protected balance breakout mounted so pins cannot be shorted accidentally

⸻

Step 3 — Bench-map all wires before soldering

Label every node.

Label set
	•	B0 through B10
	•	Main+
	•	Main-
	•	Series junction

Use heat-shrink labels or written tags.

⸻

Step 4 — Verify each 5S pack independently

Before combining them, verify:
	•	pack voltage
	•	each cell voltage from balance lead
	•	correct wire order on each pack

Record sheet

Item	Pack A	Pack B
Total voltage		
Cell 1		
Cell 2		
Cell 3		
Cell 4		
Cell 5		

Do not proceed until each pack is confirmed sane.

⸻

Step 5 — Create the permanent series link

Connect:

Pack A positive  -->  Pack B negative

This is now the internal series bridge.

Requirements
	•	proper wire gauge
	•	mechanically strain-relieved
	•	fully insulated
	•	no exposed conductor

⸻

Step 6 — Bring out main pack leads

Connect:
	•	Main negative from Pack A negative = B0
	•	Main positive from Pack B positive = B10

These become the external high-current leads.

⸻

Step 7 — Build the 10S balance harness

Map all nodes to the external balance interface:

10S Node	Source
B0	Pack A negative
B1	Pack A cell 1 tap
B2	Pack A cell 2 tap
B3	Pack A cell 3 tap
B4	Pack A cell 4 tap
B5	Pack A positive / series junction
B6	Pack B cell 1 tap
B7	Pack B cell 2 tap
B8	Pack B cell 3 tap
B9	Pack B cell 4 tap
B10	Pack B positive

Critical rule

Do not guess wire order. Confirm with a meter.

⸻

Step 8 — Verify cumulative voltage progression

With a meter referenced to B0, each balance node should increase monotonically.

Example chart

Node	Expected behavior
B0	0 V
B1	~3.7 to 4.2 V
B2	~7.4 to 8.4 V
B3	~11.1 to 12.6 V
B4	~14.8 to 16.8 V
B5	~18.5 to 21.0 V
B6	~22.2 to 25.2 V
B7	~25.9 to 29.4 V
B8	~29.6 to 33.6 V
B9	~33.3 to 37.8 V
B10	~37.0 to 42.0 V

If any step goes backward

Stop immediately. The harness is wrong.

⸻

Step 9 — Mount and protect wiring

Secure:
	•	pack bodies
	•	series bridge
	•	main leads
	•	balance harness
	•	external ports

Required protections
	•	anti-chafe sleeve where wires pass structure
	•	no unsupported solder joints
	•	no pinched balance wires
	•	no exposed midpoint

⸻

Step 10 — Perform first charger recognition test

Connect charger with:
	•	main leads
	•	balance connector

The charger should identify a 10S lithium pack.

If it identifies the wrong cell count:
	•	disconnect immediately
	•	re-check balance wire order

⸻

Step 11 — Perform low-rate first balance charge

For first commissioning:
	•	use a conservative charge rate
	•	watch pack temperature
	•	confirm charger cell count
	•	confirm end voltages are sensible

First-charge objective

Prove that:
	•	charger reads all 10 cells
	•	no cell group rises abnormally
	•	wiring remains cool
	•	connectors remain stable

